---
// The explanation below the Lab: the data, the symbols, how k-NN decides,
// how to choose k, and the model in short. Static HTML; formulas are
// rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="knn-data">
    <h2 id="knn-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      Each shape is a fruit we already know. Across the graph is its width; up
      the graph is its height, both in centimetres. Circles are apples and
      triangles are pears. Apples are round, so their width and height are
      close. Pears are taller than they are wide.
    </p>
    <p>
      The diamond is a new fruit we have not labelled yet. Its colour shows what
      the model thinks it is. The numbers are made up, and{' '}
      <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows another basket of 14 fruits.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="knn-symbols">
    <h2 id="knn-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>q</dt>
        <dd>The new fruit, the one we want to label.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>x, y</dt>
        <dd>The width and height of a known fruit.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">d</dt>
        <dd>
          The distance from the new fruit to a known fruit: the length of the
          straight line between them.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">k</dt>
        <dd>How many of the nearest fruits get a vote.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>ŷ</dt>
        <dd>The prediction: apple or pear.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="knn-how">
    <h2 id="knn-how">How the model decides</h2>
    <p>
      k-Nearest Neighbors follows a simple idea: things that look alike are
      usually the same kind. To label the new fruit, it takes three steps.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Measure:</strong> find the distance from the new fruit to every
        known fruit.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.distance} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pick the neighbours:</strong> keep the k fruits with the
        smallest distance. The dashed circle on the graph goes around them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vote:</strong> each neighbour votes for its own kind, and the
        most votes wins.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      That is all. The model does not learn a formula in advance. It simply
      remembers every fruit and looks at the closest ones each time a new fruit
      arrives.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="knn-k">
    <h2 id="knn-k">Choosing k</h2>
    <p>
      With <strong>k = 1</strong>, only the single nearest fruit decides. One
      odd fruit, like a round pear, then claims the space around it. Turn on{' '}
      <strong>Areas</strong> to see the shaded region each kind would win, and
      watch the border wiggle around single fruits.
    </p>
    <p>
      With a larger k, more fruits vote, so one odd fruit is outvoted and the
      border becomes smoother. But if k is too large, the vote reaches fruits
      far away, and small groups get drowned out.
    </p>
    <p>
      To compare values of k fairly, the score{' '}
      <strong>Right on its own data</strong> hides each known fruit in turn and
      asks its k nearest neighbours to guess it. A fruit never votes for itself.
      We use odd values of k, so with two kinds of fruit the vote can never tie.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="knn-summary">
    <h2 id="knn-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Remember</strong> every known fruit and its kind.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measure</strong> the distance from a new fruit to each of them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vote:</strong> the k nearest fruits decide the new fruit's kind.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Choose k</strong> by checking how often the model is right on
        fruits it has not seen.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
